  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe adopt a hybridized global-local optimization framework combining a structured initial guess, differential evolution for broad exploration, and SLSQP for local refinement. The core idea is to balance exploration of the solution space with improvements on the most promising candidates. This approach addresses the limitations of prior methods by using a more efficient solver for global search and enforcing constraints directly rather than relying on penalty terms. The key bottleneck is the non-convex nature of the problem, which requires a global search to avoid local optima while maintaining constraint satisfaction.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nVariables:\n- h \u2208 [0,1]^n_points with \u2211h = n_points / 2\n- Objective: minimize C\u2085 = max_k [dx * \u2211h[i] * (1 - h[i + k])] where dx = 2.0 / n_points and the sum is over valid i + k indices\n- Constraints: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1, \u2211h[i] = n_points / 2\n\nImplementation:\n- Use the exact correlation computation via np.correlate with mode=\"full\"\n- Enforce constraints via equality constraint \u2211h = n_points/2 and inequality bounds [0,1] on all entries\n- No surrogate or relaxation is used; the original objective and constraints are preserved\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nStage 1: Parent validation\n- Check if initial_h_values is valid (sum \u2248 n_points/2, entries \u2208 [0,1])\n- If available, use it as base with 3% random perturbation using np.random.normal(scale=0.03)\n- Otherwise, generate a structured pattern using Chebyshev polynomials: h[i] = 0.5 + 0.25 * np.cos(\u03c0 * i / n_points) and scale to sum to n_points/2\n\nStage 2: Global search with differential evolution\n- Initialize population: 100 candidates\n- Each candidate is a vector of h values scaled to [0,1]\n- Mutation: Blend crossover with alpha=0.5\n- Crossover: Simulated binary with probability 0.9\n- Selection: Tournament with size 5\n- Objective function: return max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\")) * dx\n- Terminate when:\n  - 500 generations passed\n  - 100 consecutive generations no improvement\n  - Time budget reached\n\nStage 3: Local refinement with SLSQP\n- Take top 5 candidates from global search\n- For each candidate:\n  - Run SLSQP optimization with:\n    - Bounds: [0,1] on all variables\n    - Equality constraint: \u2211h = n_points/2\n    - Objective: max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\")) * dx\n  - Use tight tolerances (ftol=1e-8, maxiter=300)\n  - Apply feasibility repair: clip values and adjust sum with linear interpolation\n\nStage 4: Final evaluation\n- Recompute C\u2085 for all candidates\n- Select the solution with lowest C\u2085\n- Verify constraints: \u2211h \u2248 n_points/2 (tolerance 1e-6), entries \u2208 [0,1]\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nDefault configuration:\n- n_points = 400 (balances resolution and computation time)\n- Mutation factor: 0.8\n- Crossover probability: 0.9\n- Tournament size: 5\n- DE iterations: 500\n- SLSQP iterations: 300\n- Perturbation scale: 0.03\n\nVariations:\n- When initial_h_values is available:\n  - Use it as base with 3% random perturbation\n  - Increase DE iterations to 600 for better exploration\n- When n_points < 300:\n  - Reduce DE population to 80\n  - Use 0.02 perturbation scale\n- When C\u2085 < 0.38:\n  - Increase resolution to 500 points\n  - Add penalty for high variance (\u03c3\u00b2(h) \u2264 0.1)\n- When time < 500s:\n  - Reduce DE iterations to 300\n  - Use 0.01 perturbation scale\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nTime allocation:\n- Stage 1: 10s (validation and initial pattern generation)\n- Stage 2: 300s (global search with DE)\n- Stage 3: 150s (local refinement with SLSQP)\n- Stage 4: 100s (final evaluation and selection)\n- Total: 560s (leaves 40s buffer)\n\nRobustness measures:\n- Time monitoring: Check elapsed time every 30s during optimization\n- Early stopping: Terminate DE if no improvement in 100 generations\n- Fallback: If time runs out, return the best candidate found\n- Numerical stability: Clip all values between 0 and 1 before final evaluation\n- Reconstruction: For new n_points, resample the best pattern with linear interpolation\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nFeasibility checks:\n- All returned h_values must satisfy 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n- \u2211h \u2248 n_points/2 within 1e-6 tolerance\n- No NaN or infinite values in the returned array\n\nValidation procedure:\n1. Recompute \u2211h and verify against n_points/2\n2. Check all entries are within [0,1]\n3. Recompute the correlation objective with exact dx = 2/n_points\n4. Verify that the returned c5_bound matches the computed value\n\nSanity checks:\n- Check that \u2211h is exactly n_points/2 (within 1e-9)\n- Verify that the correlation array has length 2*n_points - 1\n- Confirm that the maximum value in the correlation array is properly scaled\n- Ensure that the returned h_values are not a copy of initial_h_values",
